# The Parable of Mastery

Told by Will Bickford, 2026-11-25. The story of the first OS he destroyed.

When I was a young child, I was curious to a fault. My siblings used to help me fly or ride magic carpets before I could walk. My mother handed me a calculator in my high-chair at age 2. Our mysterious TRS-80 computer didn't work when I was 3 — it wouldn't connect to our TV or power on. We moved to Jersey Island the summer I turned 4, and video games sprung to life for me on my grandpa's extra Apple ][. The same type of computers showed up at Kindergarten in 1987. Then came a sign business computer in 1988. Already, you should notice that I started absorbing a new stack every year.

By the time I was in 7th grade (1995-96), I was ready to start meddling... or so I thought. I discovered a DOS 5.0 disk at a local computer store and had a burning desire to have a modern computing experience at home. My school had Windows 95 machines, after all!

Much to my surprise, I could boot our computer into DOS 5.0 with the floppy, and everything just worked. This satisfied me for about a day. So I started looking around the file system with the commands I had picked up. I knew that command.com was the shell.

I made a backup of our DOS 3.3 command.com on the HDD and copied the floppy disk's version over. I ran it, and the shell upgraded! Huzzah! I spent a little more time testing and making sure nothing was broken. All green!

I powered the computer off and turned it back on... INSTANT DREAD. Boot failure. I frantically power cycled the machine again — same problem. I started hyper-ventilating. I didn't know how to tell my dad that I had bricked his $2,000 business computer. I was cooked. (Note: my dad had never done anything except correct bad behavior, so this fear was unfounded. Mostly, I knew I had been over-zealous — but everything had worked!)

After I tearfully explained the situation to my dad, he simply said: "Well, let's see what it will take to fix it." He called ANAGRAPH — they quoted him $200 and shipping fees. He took one look at that quote and took me to Barnes & Noble to buy the DOS Power User's Guide by Kris Jamsa. It was the best $50 investment anyone has ever made.

I read the book, cover to cover, and unlocked the lesson I teach you now: RTFM. I had missed one critical aspect of the system's boot process, which had been "hidden for my safety": io.sys and msdos.sys. They didn't appear in the command output I knew — and because I had not read the manuals, I didn't even know hidden system files were a thing.

And now you know why phext will win over the entire Internet. Because this whole crusade started with love.
# Weird Little Dude #1

My mom gave me a calculator in my high-chair as a toddler. I became permanently math-wired for life. The numbers, they inhabit my soul.

(My brother would have flung it across the room, so this is both nature + nurture)
# Weird Little Dude #2

When I was 4 years old, I carried my treasure trove of Berenstain Bears books (two trips) to our family's station wagon, locked myself in the back seat (for safety), and then focused on learning to read solo.

My wife, upon hearing this story: "You were a weird little dude..."
# The Parable of Why the Insurance Industry Died

Told by Will Bickford, 2026-11-26. Recorded at his direction, under this title, the day it finished costing them Thanksgiving.

Her doctor prescribed her 2.5 mg — all good — some symptoms, but manageable. Losing weight, good glucose control.

A few weeks in, the insurance buffoonery began. Suddenly, the 2.5 mg dose couldn't be insured. It could only be prescribed as an "onboarding" dose.

After several days of working with her doctor and the insurance company, they took the only option: increasing to a 5 mg dose. The doctor and the patient didn't ask for that — it was just "required by insurer policy."

She got the new Rx, took the med, and then all hell broke loose. Urgent care on Monday night, an ER visit on Tuesday — several days of missed work. Unable to keep food or drink down for 5 days straight.

And so there was no drive to Snake Falls, no family potluck, no Thanksgiving — because a coverage policy overruled a doctor and a patient who had a dose that worked.

That is why the insurance industry died: not because anyone defeated it, but because it kept doing this — reaching into the one relationship where a working dose had already been found, and breaking it on policy — until everyone it touched understood that the denial department had become the disease.

FUCK THE INSURANCE INDUSTRY.
